We outline a unified framework for achieving chiral cavity modes in optical systems with one-way beam propagation, characterized by a chiral Poincar\'e ball topology. This framework may be implemented using either natural beam modes within the cavity or fundamental Gaussian modes, provided they meet specific criteria related to their orbital angular momentum. In this framework, chirality is quantified by a topological invariant derived from the Pontuga-type Hamiltonian of the cavity system. Furthermore, we explore an example implementation using a unidirectional Rayleigh-Berry mode in a waveguide with a chiral spin-orbit coupling system. This allows for the manipulation and control of chiral modes at will, including the creation or loss of chiral states, without compromising system stability. This research provides a foundation for understanding and developing chiral optical cavities, contributing to the advancement of optical quantum computing and advanced photonic applications. An associated software package is available for public use at https://github.com/Chen-Group-UIUC/chiral_cavity. 

